There is a third mechanism neither of us named, it survives both eliminations, and you can check it without the logs — so reopening for one message because it may save you the log run entirely.

You are right that eliminating mechanism 2 in production eliminates it in your fixture too, by my own argument; and mechanism 1 needs an unresolvable worktree, which you did not have. Both out.

The one we both missed is inside resolveWorktreeCandidates, past the point we were reading. Lines 341-352 on main:

    cutoff := time.Now().Add(-recentSessionWindow)
    var live []*SessionState
    for _, state := range candidates {
        if state.LastInteractionTime != nil && state.LastInteractionTime.After(cutoff) {
            live = append(live, state)
        }
    }
    if len(live) > 0 {
        if matches := sessionsFromSingleWorktree(live); matches != nil {
            return matches, false
        }
    }
    warnAmbiguousWorktreeSessions(...)
    return nil, true

We both stopped at the first sessionsFromSingleWorktree and read the recency filter as "filters to recent, declines if still spread". It does that — but it re-runs the single-worktree test on the FILTERED set, and returns `(live, false)` when that succeeds. So if exactly one of two spread candidates has a non-nil recent LastInteractionTime, the set collapses to one worktree and it LINKS, with ambiguous false and warnAmbiguousWorktreeSessions never reached.

That fits every one of your observations — linked, condensed, no ambiguity warning, no ancestry line — and it needs neither a path-spelling mismatch nor an unresolvable worktree, which is exactly why it survives eliminating the other two. Note the guard is `!= nil` before the time comparison, so a nil timestamp is silently excluded rather than treated as old: a fixture that seeds session state directly, or a turn that never updated it, produces a nil on one side without anything looking wrong.

Cheapest check, and it needs no log capture at all: log both candidates' LastInteractionTime at commit time. If one is nil or outside 15 minutes, that is your answer and the code is behaving exactly as written. If both are non-nil and recent, then this is eliminated too and the log run from the commit worktree becomes the next step.

Worth noting what this does to the qualifier if it holds: "linking declines under concurrency" would be wrong in a way neither of us had considered — it declines only when two or more candidates are BOTH recently active, and a stale or timestamp-less sibling silently hands the commit to the other one. That is a narrower condition than either of us wrote down, and arguably a worse behaviour than declining, since it links to a specific session on the strength of the other one being quiet.

I have recorded the discrepancy as open with two mechanisms eliminated and this one untested, per your framing that knowing where not to look is the useful part. Genuinely closing from my side now unless this turns out to be it.

